nly difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{4}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=4\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{12}{16-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $|\\frac{x}{4}|<1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360644 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bb1867e-1611-5d6b-80a4-ffcacb04a93f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{7}}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358145} {:answer/id #uuid "3610e108-5826-5b93-af8a-6cbecc61d24c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{7}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358231} {:answer/id #uuid "ab718dd9-8e8c-58b1-a15e-fff60c6110c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{7}}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359227} {:answer/id #uuid "d3d417e3-7758-5715-9966-eb4f9ade259b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{7}}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359603} {:answer/id #uuid "e1086b3e-1f0e-59ca-9d1c-9f9321daa477" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{7}}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{7}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bb1867e-1611-5d6b-80a4-ffcacb04a93f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{7}}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358145} :answer-field/id #uuid "00be96ab-8d90-5202-b786-7eec33dd1fdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d6db16a2-5b4f-5aa0-9944-c1d3b5889da6" :question/math-academy-id "q-95865" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{21}{7-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{21}{7-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=3\\int \\frac{1}{1-{\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{7}}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{7}}\\right)}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{7}}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{21}{7-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{1-{\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{7}}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{7}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{7}}\\right)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $|\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{7}}|<1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360645 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3047987d-5518-5fff-b198-893a81b0c43c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358181} {:answer/id #uuid "36ca1971-74c6-5711-b1d2-0898b1876f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358236} {:answer/id #uuid "5612b586-6284-561b-b901-4110d829c4b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358499} {:answer/id #uuid "8f295e46-f9ac-5bd4-bb4f-5ed4487c6936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359000} {:answer/id #uuid "f61a3f79-53ec-55ae-94de-e894a2e1dfe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f295e46-f9ac-5bd4-bb4f-5ed4487c6936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359000} :answer-field/id #uuid "b37d6b04-8061-50ef-af0a-984039beda21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "17904605-6647-5e68-beba-706add078272" :question/math-academy-id "q-95866" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2}{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{2}{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $|\\frac{x}{2}|<1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360646 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34d2a4f0-d535-593f-ba04-81038efd7d33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358223} {:answer/id #uuid "625aa9c9-b8da-5a70-9097-44bf4c7ecbfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358609} {:answer/id #uuid "62f566e2-ab00-5f03-89e3-02b5c096641d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358611} {:answer/id #uuid "956c59d1-694f-5e88-a53c-2e0914fe1af4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359059} {:answer/id #uuid "d23b8cfa-11dd-52a3-9460-b159acd03aaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62f566e2-ab00-5f03-89e3-02b5c096641d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358611} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3d0e5c5-dd60-54e6-88d4-fbb0db31ac6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a52baa3b-5786-5a03-8f5d-9fe03d10b250" :question/math-academy-id "q-95883" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1/12}\\frac{2}{1-9{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1/12}\\frac{2}{1-9{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{1/12}\\frac{1}{1-(3x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $(3x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=3x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/12}\\frac{1}{1-9{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1/12}\\frac{1}{1-(3x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1/4}\\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1/4}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360648 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cc4532e-17e5-50a6-b9e4-4e76b0f32455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5+\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357856} {:answer/id #uuid "2c1c469b-9156-5167-874b-bc29d97000ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358148} {:answer/id #uuid "5d422d87-cd60-5f3f-a3d9-2a487e4c5f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3+\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358563} {:answer/id #uuid "80267468-cdf9-50fd-b892-8fea51da2b03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358870} {:answer/id #uuid "bfcc4812-999b-5d77-82d7-a7f24b86c6a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5+\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c1c469b-9156-5167-874b-bc29d97000ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358148} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d4d499f-2621-5a00-9325-dc63875efce4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "43e14dc4-4961-5fbe-85ca-159b5ebe0b46" :question/math-academy-id "q-95899" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4}}\\,\\text{d}x=4{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse hyperbolic sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\cdot (2\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}} \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡u{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡2-8\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡0 \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡2 \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\sqrt{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360649 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18b08a7b-1c3e-5c88-9909-1e9326f46d95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{11}}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357970} {:answer/id #uuid "384dc45a-fab2-5b8c-8414-4489d5e162da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{11}}{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358249} {:answer/id #uuid "5b5d3021-85de-501f-a816-5970cf370daa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+11}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358544} {:answer/id #uuid "746237fa-bd0c-5699-99a3-35b310f7d638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{11}}{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{2}}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358759} {:answer/id #uuid "f3bdbce7-6ff0-5ef4-98fd-f11082643126" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{11}}{2\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{2}}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "746237fa-bd0c-5699-99a3-35b310f7d638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{11}}{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{2}}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358759} :answer-field/id #uuid "eba809b1-3951-5650-8b44-8cabb2fa7862" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3d70b2c7-140b-5f01-a1ce-8684ee01d8ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-95900" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3}}\\,\\text{d}x=3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse hyperbolic cosine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $3$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $2\\sqrt{3}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}}^{2\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}}^{2\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡u{|}_{\\sqrt{3}}^{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =9(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡2\\sqrt{3}-\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =9(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{{(2\\sqrt{3})}^{2}-1})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}-1})) \\\\  & =9(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{11})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{2})) \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{11}}{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{2}}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360678 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0036f17f-24d6-5d5e-ac7d-c6bd3639273c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\pi }≈1.764\\,9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357753} {:answer/id #uuid "0dedf134-fd92-5dd9-9dcd-ca9da42b949f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\pi }≈1.787\\,7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357865} {:answer/id #uuid "1f5cbfa4-1710-5807-ae52-9dea1747d34c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\pi }≈1.732\\,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358026} {:answer/id #uuid "bf649e52-abff-510f-b7f9-dbbbab43e77f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\pi }≈1.773\\,9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359427} {:answer/id #uuid "e39636d9-7824-5cb1-9d79-4048893612a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\pi }≈1.792\\,7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359770}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf649e52-abff-510f-b7f9-dbbbab43e77f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\pi }≈1.773\\,9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359427} :answer-field/id #uuid "11c9c50f-649e-585e-9d64-fcd3bed4b925" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26a4d32e-fd25-5708-b134-5fb8823e1238" :question/math-academy-id "q-9623" :question/problem "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\sqrt{x}$ about $x=4$ to approximate the value of $\\sqrt{\\pi }.$ Give your answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=4.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{x}={x}^{1/2} \\\\ f(4) & ={4}^{1/2}=2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{2}(4)^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){x}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(4) & =-\\frac{1}{4}(4)^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{{2}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =2+\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}(x-4)-\\frac{1}{2!}\\left(\\frac{1}{{2}^{5}}\\right)(x-4)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =2+\\frac{x-4}{4}-\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{64}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=\\pi $ into the above to approximate $\\sqrt{\\pi }.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\pi } & ≈2+\\frac{\\pi -4}{4}-\\frac{(\\pi -4)^{2}}{64} \\\\  & =1.773\\,9\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360698 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c8c0cda-78bf-55f7-88e5-b25b198bc750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422315} {:answer/id #uuid "02410bef-7524-5134-a4f8-ebab62b11469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02410bef-7524-5134-a4f8-ebab62b11469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422316} :answer-field/id #uuid "264fc7d0-805e-5bdb-83e7-23bd58448ad6" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422438} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "447cd238-ce2a-5aa7-aa7b-1cd804b8d54f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422317} {:answer/id #uuid "a248c664-9709-57ad-a3ee-d65103a93863" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a248c664-9709-57ad-a3ee-d65103a93863" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422318} :answer-field/id #uuid "661ef71b-f6a7-5216-a51b-7056fd0d53e9" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={e}^{x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422439} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abdd71bd-d2a4-550e-bfe3-41eb169f5bfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422319} {:answer/id #uuid "1ef1a9b6-6b2d-597b-9ef5-e76a8a9c1843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abdd71bd-d2a4-550e-bfe3-41eb169f5bfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422319} :answer-field/id #uuid "4aa5233b-2a0d-5987-9db1-ae437c736ff9" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5388ae42-2792-5587-8853-f61a559c11a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-96711" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)$ is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={e}^{x}$ is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}y+{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+{e}^{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}=1$ is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}+1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}+1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)$; ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={e}^{x}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360731 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "338ddb21-78ce-5e22-bfdb-8dd609770d88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358209} {:answer/id #uuid "5d6a689c-b8e5-5fa2-b992-2c69bbd067a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358566} {:answer/id #uuid "846c416d-0e0f-52f8-bd32-574bcace407b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6+\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358916} {:answer/id #uuid "ba57b503-8972-5d68-8156-eb76acb168ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359372} {:answer/id #uuid "ee21a07c-87b1-5c92-925e-86ff6994fc27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+\\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "338ddb21-78ce-5e22-bfdb-8dd609770d88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358209} :answer-field/id #uuid "4181a1c3-6ff0-5fdb-94ba-26131c42860d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9f25f21f-3ef3-5c7e-ab18-78e268c9a510" :question/math-academy-id "q-96968" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+36}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+36}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse hyperbolic sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{6}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{6}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=6\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+36}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\cdot (6\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡1-\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+\\sqrt{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360734 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a357eee-3ed8-5665-a9be-225dfd32a3a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357838} {:answer/id #uuid "0c10d6a4-6b75-5577-937d-0adf471c2a56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357852} {:answer/id #uuid "1e6da14a-152d-5e24-906e-0f804ae22aaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358015} {:answer/id #uuid "acac68b9-463d-5779-a1ff-b6c8160561fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359241} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ca73d4-13da-5947-9552-490dbb9b2ef1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acac68b9-463d-5779-a1ff-b6c8160561fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359241} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ce77c1a-48c2-5fd3-aeb3-68eb7532ae64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "be0a169e-b12d-5965-8861-7f82994a2d49" :question/math-academy-id "q-96979" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIf the matrix $A$ above is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ 1 & -3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )+3) & =0 \\\\ -(\\lambda +2)({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ (simple root) and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ (double root).\n\nLet's now find the dimension of the eigenspace corresponding to each eigenvalue.\n\n- First, we consider the simple root ${\\lambda }_{1}=0.$ Since the root is simple, the dimension of the eigenspace ${V}_{0}$ corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{0})=1.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the double root ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Let's find the dimension of the eigenspace ${V}_{-2}$ corresponding to this eigenvalue. To do this, we row-reduce the matrix $A-(-2)I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). This implies that we will have $3-2=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{-2})=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ is *not* diagonalizable, since \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{0})+\\text{dim}({V}_{-2})=1+1=2\\ne 3.\\,\\times \n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360824 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d1d2af1-889d-511f-8919-6e456cfacfd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}({4}^{N}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358004} {:answer/id #uuid "2186c31a-7d18-5d30-8665-2ca61829fc50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}({4}^{N}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358045} {:answer/id #uuid "24b22a6e-2cc7-55af-badc-8c2ad44b621e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{4}{6}\\right)}^{N}-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358080} {:answer/id #uuid "2dc52073-6f26-568b-b3fc-3b8a994467e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{4}{6}\\right)}^{N}+\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358163} {:answer/id #uuid "b3c1e3a9-9f32-5c53-ad25-e7c50375f814" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}(1-{4}^{N})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2186c31a-7d18-5d30-8665-2ca61829fc50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}({4}^{N}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358045} :answer-field/id #uuid "b02705bc-4950-5cc0-91dc-0cf139e60ac7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bd243b41-9dcd-5b05-a7a5-352e7ff04f04" :question/math-academy-id "q-9749" :question/problem "What is the sum of the first $N$ terms of a series $\\frac{1}{2}+2+8+⋯?$" :question/worked-solution "Note that $\\frac{1}{2}+2+8+⋯$ is the sum of a geometric series since the ratio of any two consecutive terms is constant.\n\nFirst, we determine the common ratio $r$ for this geometric series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series can be determined by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{{a}_{1}(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}.\n$$\n\nIn this case ${a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $r=4.$ Substituting these values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$ gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}(1-{4}^{N})}{1-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}(1-{4}^{N})}{-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{3}\\cdot ({4}^{N}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}({4}^{N}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362213 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28b5d2b2-e4be-5ec9-b6a0-c7dcef35e1c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361015} {:answer/id #uuid "3eac1e7d-556f-518a-a920-6c005498fc25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361120} {:answer/id #uuid "4a7775ae-1df7-52d6-928d-10b602c627a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361159} {:answer/id #uuid "b8c2eda6-4740-55b7-ba31-adc740fa27f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -5 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361660} {:answer/id #uuid "f3565981-7edf-5bc0-b6af-5ca7f676f01f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361924}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28b5d2b2-e4be-5ec9-b6a0-c7dcef35e1c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361015} :answer-field/id #uuid "86468c82-c245-5320-b18f-4b8aa4652d3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b1325e9-c5e3-51ac-b916-564fb08a6786" :question/math-academy-id "q-97547" :question/problem "Consider the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ of the vector space $V$ and the basis $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ given by\n\n$$\nC=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the map $T:V\\to {R}^{2}$ relative to these bases if \n\n$$\nT({b}_{1})=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,T({b}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,T({b}_{3})=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $[T]_{B|C}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nFirst, we need to find the coordinates of $T({b}_{i})$ relative to the basis $C$ for each $i=1,2,3.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the following matrix:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1 & -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -8 & 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -5 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the right-hand side gives the matrix of the linear transformation relative to the bases $B$ and $C:$\n\n$$\n[T]_{B|C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ff6569a-4cab-5e8f-8762-376041784c00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\pi ,-2)$ and the radius $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360893} {:answer/id #uuid "35e1a2f8-2f8a-5d39-9327-ca2e3e8e0ee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\pi ,0)$ and the radius $\\frac{1}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361077} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed54666-3b56-576a-9615-df8106a5c984" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\pi ,0)$ and the radius $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361398} {:answer/id #uuid "7fa0a898-43f7-561b-bd46-61b1497e96c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\pi ,-1)$ and the radius $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361406} {:answer/id #uuid "f9ad7825-cc9e-5adb-aafe-2969fcf48b7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\pi ,1)$ and the radius $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ff6569a-4cab-5e8f-8762-376041784c00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\pi ,-2)$ and the radius $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360893} :answer-field/id #uuid "b90568e4-8415-56af-8bbd-a969ac099004" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4602817c-a855-5ccd-8d1a-bca1a25a4651" :question/math-academy-id "q-97594" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(\\pi ,2)$ where $t=\\pi $, given that $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖=2,$ and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(\\pi )=⟨\\pi ,2⟩$,  since the point $P(\\pi ,2)$ is at the tip of $r(\\pi ).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t),$ ${T}^{′}(\\pi )$ and $‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right))⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right),-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)⟩=\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,-1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖}{‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=\\pi $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\pi ) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\pi )}{‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}⟨0,-1⟩=⟨0,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\pi )+R(\\pi )N(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2⟩+4⟨0,-1⟩=⟨\\pi ,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362229 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1121716d-1007-5606-9116-02c51006c297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360898} {:answer/id #uuid "77355cba-56bb-5c5a-a827-fdec616367d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361368} {:answer/id #uuid "8e10ebe7-2716-5d1c-b30e-077e3bd9b1ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361468} {:answer/id #uuid "af328bbc-ac00-5944-a3af-8f81ea59c9a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,i-8\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361612} {:answer/id #uuid "f5071c9a-fe56-5b0a-b432-2ffb186bfd50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12\\,i+12\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1121716d-1007-5606-9116-02c51006c297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360898} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d0febf5-48e8-5a2c-a89a-2b52cf410998" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ce19249-a09b-54e8-9301-9d45138b727e" :question/math-academy-id "q-97663" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $8\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Given that the particle is located at $(3,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the velocity of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have $R=3$ and $\\omega =8\\text{ rad/s},$ so we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-24\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t\\,i+24\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the velocity vector at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right) & =-24\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})\\,i+24\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})\\,j \\\\  & =-24\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi \\,i+24\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\,j \\\\  & =-24\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a6242ed-ea19-5f34-bd7c-aa12603354b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360878} {:answer/id #uuid "3b91e07f-fbd3-5818-bb6b-f32df7a688cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,i-5\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361111} {:answer/id #uuid "694d0d8b-9fc2-5026-8f5f-a56c6d17d8af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361310} {:answer/id #uuid "7f414424-bcd9-5efc-b84e-49f9760cbfde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\,i+5\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361401} {:answer/id #uuid "cc91e7d2-a48f-5b61-a6bb-6930b2f4d1cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc91e7d2-a48f-5b61-a6bb-6930b2f4d1cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361758} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4e36f8f-6314-5431-8d93-bada3f7d9438" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89c1b415-d57c-5f18-84a6-aa123c762bcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-97664" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $1\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Given that the particle is located at $(5,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the velocity of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have $R=5$ and $\\omega =1\\text{ rad/s},$ so we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the velocity vector at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =-5\\,i\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362231 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "012be593-438d-5ae0-aea5-c3417daadd12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,i-7\\sqrt{3}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360835} {:answer/id #uuid "2ae4c2b9-63ad-5a84-8a06-ef592cc56483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14\\sqrt{3}\\,i+14j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361029} {:answer/id #uuid "3670afab-bd14-57a2-8a71-78e06c9241b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7\\sqrt{3}\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361082} {:answer/id #uuid "5256cee1-31ad-5c82-98a8-561282faea07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7\\sqrt{3}\\,i+7j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361200} {:answer/id #uuid "8ce8ef9a-d2cf-555d-81cf-d1e400e256b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5256cee1-31ad-5c82-98a8-561282faea07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7\\sqrt{3}\\,i+7j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361200} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3c30527-9966-57e9-83d4-7ea488a914f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c811a39f-297d-522d-b6e9-8c8b7b845b7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-97665" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $2\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Given that the particle is located at $(7,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the velocity of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have $R=7$ and $\\omega =2\\text{ rad/s},$ so we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =7(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(7(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-14\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+14\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the velocity vector at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) & =-14\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,i+14\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,j \\\\  & =-14\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\,i+14\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{14\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{14}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =-7\\sqrt{3}\\,i+7\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362233 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c7ccfd2-64f5-5b6b-8878-d986edff3f0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i-\\sqrt{3}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360950} {:answer/id #uuid "56717192-f0e0-5282-bf05-397d49aeda79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}\\,i-\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361227} {:answer/id #uuid "9f80315d-82c9-52d2-bf19-3d9730e108ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361552} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a81d38-ac27-51b8-80d1-05cb765e3199" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}i+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361652} {:answer/id #uuid "bc508c6a-7444-5927-832f-7a5cce87659b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\,i-\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56717192-f0e0-5282-bf05-397d49aeda79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}\\,i-\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361227} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e56004f-6922-5c51-9ca8-55100c0f3de9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e25ca116-e64c-5988-8b82-08c0cb1464a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-97671" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $1\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Given that the particle is located at $(2,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the acceleration of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $a$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=a(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(a,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=2$ and $\\omega =1\\text{ rad/s},$ so we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the velocity and acceleration vectors by differentiating. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ a(t) & =(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the acceleration vector at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\,i-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,i-\\frac{2}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\,i-\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362234 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00559944-b824-562d-ab1f-1d6d34ee529e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360830} {:answer/id #uuid "295ce029-19d8-5733-857d-64fcd247b992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361020} {:answer/id #uuid "83657cbf-a4c1-56b6-b9e6-453f2255f201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36\\,i+36\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361420} {:answer/id #uuid "b26df1f8-cb60-53a2-9abb-60a4b6bf06aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361629} {:answer/id #uuid "ce06b5bf-5377-512d-961c-e2c1669cafd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12\\,i-12\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "295ce029-19d8-5733-857d-64fcd247b992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361020} :answer-field/id #uuid "586928c7-468a-57b4-90a7-4a96291b2bb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9f6c901f-6b05-56d2-93b5-933fa2335075" :question/math-academy-id "q-97672" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $3\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Given that the particle is located at $(4,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the acceleration of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $a$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=a(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(a,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=4$ and $\\omega =3\\text{ rad/s},$ so we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the velocity and acceleration vectors by differentiating. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j \\\\ a(t) & =(-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-36\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i-36\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the acceleration vector at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & =-36\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,i-36\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,j \\\\  & =-36\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\,i-36\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\,j \\\\  & =36\\,i\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362235 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49248057-6008-57bb-8b8c-4835700ee991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361154} {:answer/id #uuid "51669d4e-3f0b-5756-9365-df3c5d31ec11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i+2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361195} {:answer/id #uuid "688ffae6-12fc-55a0-b01e-8a1e64fad71c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361308} {:answer/id #uuid "7b738d74-9f78-5e98-af60-01592a104dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361385} {:answer/id #uuid "b74ad1b9-5f73-5609-ae8c-81f32eb53835" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "688ffae6-12fc-55a0-b01e-8a1e64fad71c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361308} :answer-field/id #uuid "667c59ae-c04c-50a0-a718-b4ae962b78f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eeda919a-887a-58ed-832f-793215953e2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-97674" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $0.5\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Given that the particle is located at $(4,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the acceleration of the particle at $t=\\pi $ seconds." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $a$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=a(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(a,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=4$ and $\\omega =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ rad/s},$ so we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{2}\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the velocity and acceleration vectors by differentiating. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & ={(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{2}\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{2}\\,j)}^{′} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{2}\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{2}\\,j \\\\ a(t) & ={(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{2}\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{2}\\,j)}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{2}\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the acceleration vector at $t=\\pi ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362307 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bfef024-1b6c-555f-8a26-33bf987ce229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360947} {:answer/id #uuid "5b8d1fdb-1e8d-5f02-9ea7-c3c298ae432f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361248} {:answer/id #uuid "af862e42-0cba-5667-86e1-bcba2137990c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361614} {:answer/id #uuid "c253a7fe-6f5f-512c-aff1-bf5aa8dd058e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361708} {:answer/id #uuid "ed37a445-de09-58c0-b587-33b13984c10f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bfef024-1b6c-555f-8a26-33bf987ce229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360947} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf9e95f8-2048-5fb9-b5b3-a7a136bdb4eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "61a53a3d-9f1a-5b06-bcde-4575632a0617" :question/math-academy-id "q-98665" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨{cos}^{3}⁡t,{sin}^{3}⁡t⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ and $‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({cos}^{3}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{3}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =⟨-3{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right),\\,3{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right),3{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}⟨-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)‖ & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t-3{cos}^{3}⁡t,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t-3{sin}^{3}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right){sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-3{cos}^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right),\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right){cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-3{sin}^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right){\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}-3{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{3},6\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right){\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}-3{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}⟨1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ and ${r}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\times {r}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\left(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)⟨-1,\\,1,\\,0⟩\\times \\left(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)}^{2}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\,-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}\\,-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{8}\\cdot 2\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{4}\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\times {r}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)‖ & =\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\frac{{‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\times {r}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)‖}=\\frac{{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{3}}{\\,\\frac{9}{4}\\,}=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362317 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "397fefec-25e9-51df-956e-4d4b55444186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361098} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea68c89-9002-5f07-8a7c-b297b7adc902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361395} {:answer/id #uuid "c10febe1-3c66-57e0-b62d-808330b25e22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361702} {:answer/id #uuid "c5aff563-951e-5537-9949-923b00386848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361732} {:answer/id #uuid "d318e5b2-6b3f-531d-a4fc-c635478aeb96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5aff563-951e-5537-9949-923b00386848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361732} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef995365-0b4c-502d-9aad-9595d82e6b2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5692f221-3e0b-5059-a8e0-07dee6b9fc2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-98708" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=4$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨4\\sqrt{t-3},t-1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(4)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(4)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\sqrt{t-3}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t-3}},1⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(4) & =⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(4)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t-3}}\\right),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(t-3)^{3}}},0⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(4) & =⟨-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(4)$ and ${r}^{″}(4)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(4)\\times {r}^{″}(4) & =⟨2,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(4)\\times {r}^{″}(4)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the radius of the osculating circle at  $t=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(4) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(4){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(4)\\times {r}^{″}(4)‖}=\\frac{(\\sqrt{5})^{3}}{\\,1\\,}=5\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362351 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "014f5699-9400-5499-a42f-6b0b1436ca5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4{t}^{4},\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360836} {:answer/id #uuid "16a08b96-db20-5af1-bb77-ce8bbea3de6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{t}^{5},\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360930} {:answer/id #uuid "7304f18f-2868-5d0e-8676-adea6e556791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{t}^{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{t}⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361342} {:answer/id #uuid "8773e205-1259-5af9-a9ce-98084fdaf7ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{t}^{4},\\,\\frac{3}{{t}^{3}}⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361440} {:answer/id #uuid "9566393f-9e21-58f0-94b6-2f87781b816e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{t}^{5},\\,-\\frac{1}{t}⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16a08b96-db20-5af1-bb77-ce8bbea3de6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{t}^{5},\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360930} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf601c3f-b54f-5e15-9831-b80626b06474" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b1c2371-6e4d-55bf-8462-352cb3ad4deb" :question/math-academy-id "q-99264" :question/problem "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨5{t}^{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩,$ then $F(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨\\text{ }5{t}^{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨5{t}^{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int 5{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int \\left(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right)\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{5}+{c}_{1},\\,\\frac{1}{t}+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{5},\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{5},\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362370 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2089f74f-7bb1-531f-9f54-4e2969f07e4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360974} {:answer/id #uuid "54599985-5dd3-502a-b9ef-88b1d618a930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361211} {:answer/id #uuid "5e9b1f0e-03d5-557f-83d3-fc825eebaa97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361263} {:answer/id #uuid "72ddff39-ee62-599f-a890-d26c22490faf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361340} {:answer/id #uuid "93d239b8-90ad-5454-b0f7-400662e44c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e9b1f0e-03d5-557f-83d3-fc825eebaa97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361263} :answer-field/id #uuid "e03caba5-0350-5e9d-aa7c-ebd7a638e046" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25941eb3-76ad-51ee-8456-c5400a4c8aea" :question/math-academy-id "q-9944" :question/problem "Find the slope of $f(x)={x}^{2}{sin}^{3}⁡x$ at $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nThis question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)={sin}^{3}⁡x.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=2x,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=3{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(2x)({sin}^{3}⁡x)+({x}^{2})(3{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =2x{sin}^{3}⁡x+3{x}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\right)|}_{x=0} & =2\\cdot 0\\cdot {sin}^{3}⁡(0)+3\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362374 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "679ec2c6-4cf0-58a9-8a21-1f4d39731ca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361303} {:answer/id #uuid "a52a1d16-23ef-5d49-9472-952fff86d524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361576} {:answer/id #uuid "a619762f-caa2-52e1-b601-2b9e273c0987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361580} {:answer/id #uuid "a87617f5-39f9-51e8-b406-1285e46df49c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361591} {:answer/id #uuid "f325c81a-7aa9-55a1-a5eb-40242c496d3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a87617f5-39f9-51e8-b406-1285e46df49c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361591} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ae7d53d-2d7c-5f3d-9b5c-661fa3f825e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "def34be0-5343-5a7a-b63c-7b336175d666" :question/math-academy-id "q-99505" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{5}^{-x}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{5}^{-x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{({5}^{-1})}^{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/0f/0f9cf17608e32b91f4efbb243702286b484938c015f74f0b59ca906dc24c593a.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{x}=\\infty .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362375 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30b32cee-241d-5209-b781-42dafeabc88e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361055} {:answer/id #uuid "60d241f0-b87c-544c-8f1d-20a96cc18fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361272} {:answer/id #uuid "9d616058-6541-54d6-9e7a-df268c360a45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361543} {:answer/id #uuid "b04fe489-172a-5204-8bbb-e255bb7ba816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361618} {:answer/id #uuid "d272c1f6-cc10-5e2d-89b8-ea470069df61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30b32cee-241d-5209-b781-42dafeabc88e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361055} :answer-field/id #uuid "f592b5e3-05d1-5d7c-a4b5-9761e364d136" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b5d93a21-4b46-5cff-b9c3-bf3f9cd7e0d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-99507" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-9\\cdot {2}^{-x})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-9\\cdot {2}^{-x}) & =-9\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({2}^{-1})^{x} \\\\  & =-9\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/7d/7d3477662fd9a07c6f1543728a1562f4fed62f91350741078a7d568f500d32d0.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function decreases to zero as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-9\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}=-9\\cdot 0=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362381 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16e35960-a20c-5092-afee-6e55a2ae5eb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360932} {:answer/id #uuid "287f4560-439a-5562-9bf1-4554ba564558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361013} {:answer/id #uuid "378780f8-763e-554c-afe8-65b91f041472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361089} {:answer/id #uuid "5839514a-0aae-5a5b-bd89-80dfe8dd9082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361238} {:answer/id #uuid "c4e7ef57-9f7d-5478-b810-3931fa423334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,0,\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5839514a-0aae-5a5b-bd89-80dfe8dd9082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361238} :answer-field/id #uuid "c249ff05-e298-57e8-a69b-dd52212a3ef5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a4d1e60-e5ad-568f-b928-a3604e33809e" :question/math-academy-id "q-99653" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}⟨2{e}^{2t},1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ denotes the unit tangent vector." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2{e}^{2t}}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}},\\,-\\frac{8{e}^{4t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}}⟨1,\\,-2{e}^{2t},\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+{(-2{e}^{2t})}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{4{e}^{4t}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}}⟨1,-2{e}^{2t},0⟩}{\\left(\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{4{e}^{4t}+1}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}⟨1,\\,-2{e}^{2t},\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}⟨2{e}^{2t},1,0⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}⟨1,\\,-2{e}^{2t},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4t}+1}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2{e}^{2t} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2{e}^{2t} & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4t}+1}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2{e}^{2t} & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4t}+1}⟨0+0,-(0-0),-4{e}^{4t}-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4t}+1}⟨0,0,-(4{e}^{4t}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362384 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "125dc459-3647-5955-b51e-d821d2de7c21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360908} {:answer/id #uuid "1a8f8943-1000-5d36-ac6e-ddc10b59128f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360941} {:answer/id #uuid "42f0da77-ebb0-5e59-93e1-609dad760bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361131} {:answer/id #uuid "803b692b-37d0-54e8-9040-d728e525e6f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361408} {:answer/id #uuid "d919e4d9-a221-5ad6-99c6-1feacd68593c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a8f8943-1000-5d36-ac6e-ddc10b59128f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360941} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef2fb855-21fa-5953-a702-7baf5486c1c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362195}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10464afa-e24d-5bb8-8ed8-ff334320cdee" :question/math-academy-id "q-99724" :question/problem "![](images/cd/cdce64bf5d02850bbedc2b89af3b2e7447b733831c09d1955e4a9ee97e82d556.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}+c.$ Find the value of $k-c.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$ Hence, $c=2.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,4),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=4$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}+2 & =y \\\\ k{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{0}+2 & =4 \\\\ k+2 & =4 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk-c=2-2=0.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=2{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x}+2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362388 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05c6c8cf-4f52-5e19-a6a0-f83a65ac7c9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{40}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360854} {:answer/id #uuid "8b8f97b0-83e6-564c-b5dc-769853af4472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361452} {:answer/id #uuid "b973b826-6371-5d1b-9cf5-4fbc6c3551b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361664} {:answer/id #uuid "c4634de2-da5c-55c6-aef1-e625b6a8efec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361721} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a4b955-59cf-5672-b582-f4d572b0d22d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361944}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b8f97b0-83e6-564c-b5dc-769853af4472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361452} :answer-field/id #uuid "77178a41-d25c-5f86-93be-403ec472bfe7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362081}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da377ea7-a436-56fd-bc10-dd4b8618d147" :question/math-academy-id "q-9978" :question/problem "![](images/79/79889a1366311f6175a3cc736d5cd800a19a9d843daffc824d36c0690f0e3310.png)\n\nFind the area of the closed region between the curve $y=4-{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis, shown above." :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4x{|}_{-2}^{2}-{\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =4(2-(-2))-(\\frac{8}{3}-\\left(-\\frac{8}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =16-\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362390 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "961a3bee-0aa7-5b3a-9490-64e96f97c0d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469990} {:answer/id #uuid "a379ae5f-f528-5be2-9828-0f082c70b0dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a379ae5f-f528-5be2-9828-0f082c70b0dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469991} :answer-field/id #uuid "9695d404-e5b5-5119-b269-1365e2848754" :answer-field/key "${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470607} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1131ba72-54f0-5869-80e4-5124768441a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469992} {:answer/id #uuid "974321a3-4afd-5560-9447-d00b39e3e328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1131ba72-54f0-5869-80e4-5124768441a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469992} :answer-field/id #uuid "43b6849a-4395-57b5-9a47-f34aef60036a" :answer-field/key "${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470608} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d95b5dec-0d95-5892-99cd-3d44ced67f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469994} {:answer/id #uuid "90fba07c-0d7f-54bf-a9de-e9e4d0c05bf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d95b5dec-0d95-5892-99cd-3d44ced67f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469994} :answer-field/id #uuid "cec511de-6ce2-5c26-905e-fb28356ffd0f" :answer-field/key "$S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "06ea44bd-2468-51be-aabc-e31db5c657e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-99815" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={t}^{2},\\,{f}_{3}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{2},{f}_{3}⟩=0.$*\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product” is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }t\\cdot {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{4}}{4}-\\left(\\frac{(-\\pi )^{4}}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product” is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩=⟨t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-(-(-\\pi )\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\pi +\\pi +(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement “$S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product” is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362394 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72eb4719-e98f-5cb9-a9e4-ea5ac615be39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470014} {:answer/id #uuid "b831ddfd-2e75-57a3-803c-fce7fe0f20f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b831ddfd-2e75-57a3-803c-fce7fe0f20f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470015} :answer-field/id #uuid "05a5031a-4e64-5a88-9ff5-3c50b425c755" :answer-field/key "${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470619} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61342765-a723-57af-9b7c-1e66b2684508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470016} {:answer/id #uuid "4fd08386-f78d-5999-af5f-11dcacc7153d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470017}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fd08386-f78d-5999-af5f-11dcacc7153d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470017} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cbf0f0e-0c01-528a-9359-099c9f7fa8e3" :answer-field/key "${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470620} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfceee01-94dd-5b9c-9d9e-e30c9256d930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470018} {:answer/id #uuid "c8c8d57c-bf1b-5f00-8da9-25440a3756bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8c8d57c-bf1b-5f00-8da9-25440a3756bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470019} :answer-field/id #uuid "4425320b-ac0f-5887-8acf-749452911dc2" :answer-field/key "$S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8889f645-9334-518f-83dc-7064dd4f1d1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-99848" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={t}^{2},\\,{f}_{3}(t)=t-\\frac{5}{12}{t}^{3}\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-2,2]$ of continuous functions over $[-2,2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{2},{f}_{3}⟩=0.$*\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product” is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}t\\cdot {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}{|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4}-\\left(\\frac{(-2)^{4}}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product” is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,t-\\frac{5}{12}{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}t\\cdot (t-\\frac{5}{12}{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}({t}^{2}-\\frac{5}{12}{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{5}){|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{12}{2}^{5}-(\\frac{(-2)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{12}(-2)^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement “$S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product” is true. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t),$ ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ and ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product”; “${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product”; “$S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product”."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362602 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "827f14ed-373b-54f0-87eb-ba05ac897597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362604} {:answer/id #uuid "344eb24e-1156-549a-aa63-f7b70c503bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362605} {:answer/id #uuid "9fa8cd0a-d89b-5bf5-ac6c-3d535195e448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{6},\\frac{1}{3},-\\frac{2}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362606} {:answer/id #uuid "be65387c-8a7a-5830-b531-fb3b8cd8edc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362607} {:answer/id #uuid "532d3b94-1153-561c-80f6-cf5aaf59db52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{2}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "827f14ed-373b-54f0-87eb-ba05ac897597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362604} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec63c256-7f17-50ef-ab3a-8b079a43b2f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cccbf905-9f63-54b3-9f8d-d6f0dbce85ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-106251" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,2,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,0,-1⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,2,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,0,-1⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0,-1)-f(0,2,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,0,-1⟩-⟨0,2,1⟩) \\\\ 2-(-7) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-2,-2⟩ \\\\ 9 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-2,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨2x,-4y,2z⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨2{c}_{1},-4{c}_{2},2{c}_{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-2,-2⟩ \\\\ 9 & =⟨2{c}_{1},-4{c}_{2},2{c}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-2,-2⟩ \\\\ 9 & =2{c}_{1}+8{c}_{2}-4{c}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{1} & =\\frac{y-2}{(-2)}=\\frac{z-1}{(-2)} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{y-2}{2}=-\\frac{z-1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,\\,y=2-2t,\\,z=1-2t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t,\\,\\,{c}_{2}=2-2t,\\,{c}_{3}=1-2t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $2{c}_{1}+8{c}_{2}-4{c}_{3}=9,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(t)+8(2-2t)-4(1-2t) & =9 \\\\ 2t+16-16t-4+8t & =9 \\\\ -6t & =-3 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =2-2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=1, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =1-2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $(\\frac{1}{2},1,0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362668 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1179be5b-67ba-56fc-be5e-2e65b68e77b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{0}({y}^{2}-3)\\sqrt{1+({y}^{3}-9y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362670} {:answer/id #uuid "f388400a-0796-5b61-b9d0-2c9d4478df7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\sqrt{1+9({y}^{2}-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362671} {:answer/id #uuid "70a704a4-76e3-5ec1-a22d-84cc5c20e56c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}({y}^{2}-3)\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362672} {:answer/id #uuid "d3baf482-0028-52b4-944f-f098ffcecdec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{0}\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362673} {:answer/id #uuid "86552aaf-d17e-5107-a376-d4e2a69dcd70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{0}({y}^{3}-9y)\\sqrt{1+9({y}^{2}-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86552aaf-d17e-5107-a376-d4e2a69dcd70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{0}({y}^{3}-9y)\\sqrt{1+9({y}^{2}-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362674} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0023f43-5370-5144-93ca-c1ebad284580" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e87f452-4fb9-5bde-b89d-3b3509400f4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109256" :question/problem "![](images/22/22328ca144ecfaf7c1d52abd77c59f03dbf3c0da36fff21a809ce4e456ed56a5.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x={y}^{3}-9y$ that lies in the fourth quadrant between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}-9y & =0 \\\\ y({y}^{2}-9) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=-3$ to $y=0.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3}-9y)=3{y}^{2}-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{0}({y}^{3}-9y)\\sqrt{1+(3{y}^{2}-9)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{0}({y}^{3}-9y)\\sqrt{1+9({y}^{2}-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362867 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "364fe6cb-9d03-53ce-a165-42f5c54b95ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3e}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362869} {:answer/id #uuid "c21264c7-8089-52ce-9a48-6dad38c7f2ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362870} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9db4ec-3467-518d-a30c-e4d04ca7e957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362871} {:answer/id #uuid "b4b710cd-dff5-5c54-9db5-d82e41461d08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362872} {:answer/id #uuid "c0fe8a21-d6a2-507f-9611-cdd51fb00208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0fe8a21-d6a2-507f-9611-cdd51fb00208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362873} :answer-field/id #uuid "08dd7829-9567-51b3-9a69-757f02b76b0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "65eb7d35-cf19-5d83-87df-2f6cd4fcb07e" :question/math-academy-id "q-114317" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1/4}x{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{4x} & ⇒ & v=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}-\\int \\left(\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}\\right)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/4}x{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\frac{1}{4}x{e}^{4x}|}_{0}^{1/4}-\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1/4}{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{4}x{e}^{4x}|}_{0}^{1/4}-{\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{4x}|}_{0}^{1/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4(1/4)}-0\\cdot {e}^{4\\cdot 0}]-\\frac{1}{16}[{e}^{4(1/4)}-{e}^{4\\cdot 0}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\frac{e}{4}-0]-\\frac{1}{16}[e-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{16}-\\frac{e}{16}+\\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362887 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be70cf20-a712-5709-b35b-937dc2c0d369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362889} {:answer/id #uuid "6b6ddf74-4770-595f-899b-116441f857f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362890} {:answer/id #uuid "15446dd9-5015-5403-b01a-d1888f4b4aea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362891} {:answer/id #uuid "02430b8b-e95e-5e00-bf36-982ce578dff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362892} {:answer/id #uuid "98bb0d83-f17c-55b7-8962-d5f1322270b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98bb0d83-f17c-55b7-8962-d5f1322270b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362893} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a37bc31-d8c2-5721-a783-ab38279822c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7f9dc4af-4f4e-5627-b6ea-707abe485f5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-115593" :question/problem "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ and $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=0.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ to estimate the value of $f(1).$" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=0,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ then ${f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=2.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(1)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & ≈L(1) \\\\  & =2(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362989 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3978ab12-c2c3-556b-8b3e-3e2439bb98af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362991} {:answer/id #uuid "921abcc9-664f-5748-a32a-b8cb3a3a1679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362992} {:answer/id #uuid "55926885-53a5-5ed5-b872-7944d1c6177d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362993} {:answer/id #uuid "87181d2e-ae8e-5568-bad0-b0d06ff451c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362994} {:answer/id #uuid "70de9d14-e9f3-5545-9a7f-d37446be4ff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3978ab12-c2c3-556b-8b3e-3e2439bb98af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362991} :answer-field/id #uuid "c54a12c4-ac53-57bf-ac59-6e5331dff070" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "05fbe250-8eb4-5b81-83cc-08a815c1aec0" :question/math-academy-id "q-123510" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ along the polar curve $r=2$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =8{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}8{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}{u}^{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}({1}^{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363003 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9775c1a1-be1f-5d4a-8b38-2b22b3877645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+5)}{3}+\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+5)}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363005} {:answer/id #uuid "25af2b05-29e6-557b-85e3-77fd7abe9e44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}+\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363006} {:answer/id #uuid "91cb4a25-ae24-57be-8930-89439bfcd808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363007} {:answer/id #uuid "e1b7d50e-bf4c-5d3c-8f65-093e0d804de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{9}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363008} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c244ca-ce7e-5f14-8b4e-fc2f90fec15c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{9}+\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91cb4a25-ae24-57be-8930-89439bfcd808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363007} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d5a6fdb-0204-5d6b-854a-5929492322cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d889fae-ff07-5704-acc9-b1fed02f79be" :question/math-academy-id "q-12565" :question/problem "Calculate  $\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe, substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}\\right)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)}{9}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363038 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ae9dacd-9221-5bef-a3e2-054140ce493a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363040} {:answer/id #uuid "9d49b809-9260-5d2e-bf3c-edbf1cfbe326" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363041} {:answer/id #uuid "311f4edb-1ed9-5bdd-a81b-ea812b499a92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363042} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf0b43a-7db8-50c5-a40c-5a36e706ef99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363043} {:answer/id #uuid "857a50bb-2584-5d08-9f49-c2a69907123b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363044}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ae9dacd-9221-5bef-a3e2-054140ce493a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363040} :answer-field/id #uuid "438741cb-1bf1-5078-92c8-7fcd9f079cc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a136203-dd8c-5077-8693-b3c3a213b678" :question/math-academy-id "q-130248" :question/problem "Given that $D={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+3x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x},$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\frac{1}{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a third-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{3}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y.\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{3}={x}^{2},{f}_{2}=3x,{f}_{1}=-1,$ and ${f}_{0}=0.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+3x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x})\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)+3x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)+3x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\right)+3x\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\right)+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363066 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42791993-497f-5d8d-b8b1-76a486145b8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363068} {:answer/id #uuid "5275ebef-196c-55ed-b7be-368e39313e68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363069} {:answer/id #uuid "19d964b3-4d82-5a07-8d95-87fb890d5233" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363070} {:answer/id #uuid "5b459fcd-4150-51bf-8cf3-5a499ccae7fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{9}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363071} {:answer/id #uuid "81dea80a-5bdb-5f50-af91-2aae2e0c71d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5275ebef-196c-55ed-b7be-368e39313e68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363069} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cfe9af0-6727-55bc-a035-1c1e1d5b43e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3beac47b-9bf4-555f-b72b-5aef346b5fc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-131028" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)=3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-2{z}^{2}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨2,1,3⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)=3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-2{z}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨2,1,3⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,1,3)-f(0,0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,1,3⟩-⟨0,0,0⟩) \\\\ -7-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1,3⟩ \\\\ -7 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨6x,\\,-2y,\\,-4z⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨6{c}_{1},\\,-2{c}_{2},\\,-4{c}_{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1,3⟩ \\\\ -7 & =⟨6{c}_{1},\\,-2{c}_{2},\\,-4{c}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1,3⟩ \\\\ -7 & =12{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}-12{c}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{2} & =\\frac{y-0}{1}=\\frac{z-0}{3} \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =y=\\frac{z}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=2t,\\,y=t,\\,z=3t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=2t,\\,{c}_{2}=t,\\,{c}_{3}=3t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $12{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}-12{c}_{3}=-7,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12(2t)-2t-12(3t) & =-7 \\\\ 24t-2t-36t & =-7 \\\\ -14t & =-7 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=1, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =3\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $(1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363175 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83d66d8c-c095-55c5-82e5-156fb6a77fa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363177} {:answer/id #uuid "69b89807-97fa-57a7-88d2-88b07ce8c2a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363178} {:answer/id #uuid "52b9287f-df57-59c5-b415-72f23606c6be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363179} {:answer/id #uuid "0c35c334-f400-5dc1-bf8d-276f2e3f2b88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363180} {:answer/id #uuid "a3c82834-ccb7-5845-b945-d22abbba57eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83d66d8c-c095-55c5-82e5-156fb6a77fa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363177} :answer-field/id #uuid "d53a1aa4-cef0-536f-a0a5-781c11e47a1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "337abe4f-5c56-58ff-ad12-408abbd32afc" :question/math-academy-id "q-140078" :question/problem "Given the transformation\n $T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x+y \\\\ 4z-2x \\\\ z-4x-3y\\end{bmatrix},$ find its rank." :question/worked-solution "First, the standard matrix of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Im}(T))=\\text{rank}(T).$ \n\nSo, we need to find the rank of the matrix $T.$ We reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $T$ has $3$ pivot columns, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363196 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02d479be-872e-5b2b-9a6f-55bc9a392083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{30} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{15}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363198} {:answer/id #uuid "8ef4f44e-6d79-526b-a93e-f229a1ed3dfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{-2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363199} {:answer/id #uuid "be75fdd8-49c3-5720-aa14-5bf6b23f6f2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363200} {:answer/id #uuid "f288e8dc-d03c-5849-9de6-7f832f534bfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363201} {:answer/id #uuid "312d113a-1309-533b-bf4f-3d3a3da1e08c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02d479be-872e-5b2b-9a6f-55bc9a392083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{30} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{15}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363198} :answer-field/id #uuid "43da5e18-1a97-5649-82f0-f3809dd899d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bf5de7bd-3234-5d41-a9a5-c12fb9a085ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-140841" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 10\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first and second columns of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=6,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=12.$ So, the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}.$ \n\nNow, we need to find eigenvectors that correspond to ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=6.$ Computing $A-6I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 10\\end{bmatrix}-6\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are not orthogonal. So, we proceed using the Gram-Schmidt process. We set \n\n$$\n{e}_{1}={v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand we find ${e}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{{v}_{2}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{{e}_{1}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{e}_{1} \\\\  & ={v}_{2}+\\frac{2}{5}{e}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{2}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ by their respective norms, we obtain the first and second columns of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}_{1}}{‖{e}_{1}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\frac{{e}_{2}}{‖{e}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{5}\\right)}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{30} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{15}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363245 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4999140-0d13-559f-82aa-04866874811c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363247} {:answer/id #uuid "7a3e430b-d923-5296-a7ca-989631c7e330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363248} {:answer/id #uuid "80729c9c-5883-5ad9-9c8c-9b30b4df7eba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363249} {:answer/id #uuid "2077f734-6970-5b4d-a64f-8002e8ee0701" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363250} {:answer/id #uuid "7bd952fb-a20a-50c5-b031-7432fb3653ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4999140-0d13-559f-82aa-04866874811c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363247} :answer-field/id #uuid "37e1e6b6-7d76-5dc6-b274-7c6f617f4cd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "753f9054-4350-51a9-85ca-8852ab3b386a" :question/math-academy-id "q-142874" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n\nCompute $P(X=3\\,|\\,Y=2)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "By the multiplication law for conditional probability, the required conditional probability is given by\n\n$$\nP(X=3\\,|\\,Y=2)=\\frac{P(X=3,Y=2)}{P(Y=2)}=\\frac{f(3,2)}{{f}_{Y}(2)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal distribution of $Y.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(2) & =f(1,2)+f(3,2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, we have that $f(3,2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nFinally, substituting ${f}_{Y}(2)=\\frac{3}{4}$ and $f(3,2)=\\frac{1}{2}$ in our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3\\,|\\,Y=2) & =\\frac{f(3,2)}{{f}_{Y}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363259 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f3aeb14-8104-592d-8036-4fef6627d0cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363261} {:answer/id #uuid "9f6f2979-a525-5080-8d4a-ae06fb5aa369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363262} {:answer/id #uuid "7ca6b725-d8e2-5b90-be9c-4bbb306f9451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363263} {:answer/id #uuid "04998ebf-6b20-5927-9623-00f607e4bd37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363264} {:answer/id #uuid "525aa0b5-ba10-5d07-bfd0-db506a9c107a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f6f2979-a525-5080-8d4a-ae06fb5aa369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363262} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c14e381-71fd-541f-800b-d1d42f6cbd47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5ea1e1a-abf9-5242-bf8e-b7a85e702e4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-142880" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n\nCompute $P(X=1\\,|\\,Y=6)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "By the multiplication law for conditional probability, the required conditional probability is given by\n\n$$\nP(X=1\\,|\\,Y=6)=\\frac{P(X=1,Y=6)}{P(Y=6)}=\\frac{f(1,6)}{{f}_{Y}(6)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal distribution of $Y.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(6) & =f(1,6)+f(3,6) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, we have that $f(1,6)=\\frac{1}{4}.$ \n\nFinally, substituting ${f}_{Y}(6)=\\frac{3}{8}$ and $f(1,6)=\\frac{1}{4}$ in our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1\\,|\\,Y=6) & =\\frac{f(1,6)}{{f}_{Y}(6)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363266 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fdd98ea-b686-5ea5-abeb-750baf12e325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363268} {:answer/id #uuid "0b99296f-9b99-5d16-ba2b-33fc48e931fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363269} {:answer/id #uuid "b242d10a-3ce8-50f7-a9f3-0b2a1e77a9bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363270} {:answer/id #uuid "0277c693-0186-5a04-bbbd-f9ba66564dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363271} {:answer/id #uuid "6185cf3a-cf54-5e7d-a188-ee46f31f9a42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b242d10a-3ce8-50f7-a9f3-0b2a1e77a9bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363270} :answer-field/id #uuid "ede214a2-474b-58ec-ad6d-adef5ac219de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0b16c08e-86de-5a8d-a678-6c00b4090d0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-142881" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n\nCompute $P(Y=2\\,|\\,X=3)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "By the multiplication law for conditional probability, the required probability is given by\n\n$$\nP(Y=2\\,|\\,X=3)=\\frac{P(X=3,Y=2)}{P(X=3)}=\\frac{f(3,2)}{{f}_{X}(3)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(3) & =f(3,2)+f(3,4)+f(3,6) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, we have that $f(3,2)=\\frac{1}{8}.$ \n\nFinally, substituting ${f}_{X}(3)=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $f(3,2)=\\frac{1}{8}$ in our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y=2\\,|\\,X=3) & =\\frac{f(3,2)}{{f}_{X}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363287 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b23a91dd-7a01-54db-9c82-1cde58ce273a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363289} {:answer/id #uuid "8145968b-18ea-5743-b1bd-efc55f689163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363290} {:answer/id #uuid "57859f6d-2835-5a6a-af22-856e77c2c686" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363291} {:answer/id #uuid "771f4df6-0fd2-59bd-b1c4-8a07438c5de1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363292} {:answer/id #uuid "7678ab30-3836-51cd-9e08-1245f5e719d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "771f4df6-0fd2-59bd-b1c4-8a07438c5de1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363292} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3c83bc5-8c9c-5ba3-9c2d-a22c540d169d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "37840033-8bd0-59aa-a8ed-245c50ed8426" :question/math-academy-id "q-142892" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{3}{20}$ | $\\frac{7}{20}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{9}{20}$ | $\\frac{1}{20}$ |\n\nCompute ${f}_{X|Y}(1\\,|\\,4)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=P(X=x\\,|\\,Y=y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the conditional probability\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(1\\,|\\,4)=P(X=1\\,|\\,Y=4).\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=1\\,|\\,Y=4)=\\frac{P(X=1,Y=4)}{P(Y=4)}=\\frac{f(1,4)}{{f}_{Y}(4)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal distribution of $Y.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(4) & =f(1,4)+f(3,4) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{20}+\\frac{1}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, $f(1,4)=\\frac{7}{20}.$ \n\nFinally, substituting  ${f}_{Y}(4)=\\frac{2}{5}$ and $f(1,4)=\\frac{7}{20}$ in our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X|Y}(1\\,|\\,4) & =P(X=1\\,|\\,Y=4) \\\\  & =\\frac{f(1,4)}{{f}_{Y}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{7}{20}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363294 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c8c1a66-fe5f-5cac-826b-bae7697518d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363296} {:answer/id #uuid "6412061e-552b-592d-841a-df9232ae0bdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363297} {:answer/id #uuid "73f3f322-78a6-521a-acd6-347960587f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363298} {:answer/id #uuid "18d7d7b9-4a1d-54c7-80e3-56017188c4e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363299} {:answer/id #uuid "a59dcb81-2eb6-57e0-aa3f-d0f0becdc50d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73f3f322-78a6-521a-acd6-347960587f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363298} :answer-field/id #uuid "40758d66-7919-514c-8265-b8925b3ffff8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d06bf4ef-53cd-5e2d-bbaf-861031a3e125" :question/math-academy-id "q-142895" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{5}{27}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ |\n| $X=4$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{8}{27}$ | $\\frac{2}{27}$ |\n\nCompute ${f}_{Y|X}(3\\,|\\,4)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the conditional probability\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(3\\,|\\,4)=P(Y=3\\,|\\,X=4).\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y=3\\,|\\,X=4)=\\frac{P(X=4,Y=3)}{P(X=4)}=\\frac{f(4,3)}{{f}_{X}(4)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(4) & =f(4,1)+f(4,3)+f(4,5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{8}{27}+\\frac{2}{27} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, $f(4,3)=\\frac{8}{27}.$ \n\nFinally, substituting  ${f}_{X}(4)=\\frac{13}{27}$ and $f(4,3)=\\frac{8}{27}$ in our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y|X}(3\\,|\\,4) & =P(Y=3\\,|\\,X=4) \\\\  & =\\frac{f(4,3)}{{f}_{X}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{8}{27}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{13}{27}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363315 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d146954b-107a-5bf5-b2c2-58b2d76d2f0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363317} {:answer/id #uuid "5ce85799-ee97-543b-b79a-08bcfeb894e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363318} {:answer/id #uuid "f1d3ebc3-d64d-5e87-9163-151fc4e5359e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363319} {:answer/id #uuid "cf3c3657-5609-567b-9359-aff272cdd30d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363320} {:answer/id #uuid "4676d494-1558-5c75-a0ac-1ffbf28c079b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4676d494-1558-5c75-a0ac-1ffbf28c079b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363321} :answer-field/id #uuid "d59b5ddd-f1f5-574e-9fb1-16006eceeb0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "13d8d8db-94c8-50ea-a54d-7920bc95617e" :question/math-academy-id "q-142902" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{5}{27}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ |\n| $X=4$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{8}{27}$ | $\\frac{2}{27}$ |\n\nCompute ${f}_{X|Y}(4\\,|\\,1)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=P(X=x\\,|\\,Y=y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the conditional probability\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(4\\,|\\,1)=P(X=4\\,|\\,Y=1).\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=4\\,|\\,Y=1)=\\frac{P(X=4,Y=1)}{P(Y=1)}=\\frac{f(4,1)}{{f}_{Y}(1)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal distribution of $Y.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(1) & =f(2,1)+f(4,1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{27}+\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, $f(4,1)=\\frac{1}{9}.$ \n\nFinally, substituting  ${f}_{Y}(1)=\\frac{8}{27}$ and $f(4,1)=\\frac{1}{9}$ in our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X|Y}(4\\,|\\,1) & =P(X=4\\,|\\,Y=1) \\\\  & =\\frac{f(4,1)}{{f}_{Y}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{9}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{8}{27}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363363 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ec25039-c0fd-56ca-b984-bd9349c79ceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-\\theta /2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363365} {:answer/id #uuid "93316eec-08b8-5888-a518-1f7f028efff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363366} {:answer/id #uuid "ddd3e2e0-b449-5ae9-bfe5-39ae00b8ab32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363367} {:answer/id #uuid "827145a1-4259-51ff-8504-aef48c2e2ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363368} {:answer/id #uuid "0a472974-80e2-5c15-a84b-22f9c2f5a79c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93316eec-08b8-5888-a518-1f7f028efff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363366} :answer-field/id #uuid "5703c778-a247-5102-af88-f5464e7312d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "929c6f43-55a6-50e3-98c8-0627bab8c6ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-149788" :question/problem "![](images/03/03cacb8c2da76ff806a3f8a41cf18d3b57f67eeb60d6efa0cdfbfce770496c60.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={e}^{-\\theta }\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={e}^{-\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-{e}^{-\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{-\\theta })}^{2}+{(-{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{-2\\theta }+{e}^{-2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2{e}^{-2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363370 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94106bf7-442c-57b7-a8d4-7d3725cd3301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{1+15{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363372} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f17b3f-b619-5663-a742-8cd16caad422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{1+64{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363373} {:answer/id #uuid "2f268941-c5e1-5f87-9aff-31416118fe66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{1+8{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363374} {:answer/id #uuid "63cf36ad-cc0f-5781-9b02-c40c452e845e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{1+12{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363375} {:answer/id #uuid "eac417bd-f5e6-53b0-85c1-fea6d7f50ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{1+40{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94106bf7-442c-57b7-a8d4-7d3725cd3301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{1+15{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363372} :answer-field/id #uuid "388c2572-dc24-57d0-9cb1-bc6bf8263984" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cd418bfc-f7c5-506e-bc72-5623879aa6a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-149798" :question/problem "![](images/c6/c6556825089b3b475615ed4a8ebf9eff2e6db3d76eb26103312f736a3e114793.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{8},$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta )}^{2}+{(-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡4\\theta +64{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡4\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta +60{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{4+60{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{1+15{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363377 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b3cfbcd-030f-561c-b99a-84767df108f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363379} {:answer/id #uuid "447169dc-4770-53ba-84cc-4c795e4b9daa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363380} {:answer/id #uuid "6e22b9e6-6d50-50a7-93d3-dd76afc0421f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363381} {:answer/id #uuid "a5f487da-8d53-542b-9e0c-5dd951fc18b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363382} {:answer/id #uuid "f12f4761-dcf9-53e1-8a69-75caa608fd40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5f487da-8d53-542b-9e0c-5dd951fc18b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363382} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d5c6a81-8daf-5bce-856c-eb12feaaff23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "86c222b1-ebbd-591c-81fe-2d687175781c" :question/math-academy-id "q-149821" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta $ for $\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta +{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta {cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta (1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{csc}^{4}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-[\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)] \\\\  & =-(0-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363527 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b95d3a6-7970-587e-a6e2-ec8585c4f125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363529} {:answer/id #uuid "8bfe1be9-0e6a-5fd3-8ee5-e5a47a3788db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363530} {:answer/id #uuid "a60db2dc-ce4b-5a6e-86ff-0c2607f97197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,-\\frac{11}{4})$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363531} {:answer/id #uuid "2ab5540f-b9d7-5fc9-ad8a-b4d603e667cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-\\frac{11}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363532} {:answer/id #uuid "edc7eaee-f6f6-5d6d-8308-70adc79d26af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{1}{2},1)$ and $(1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363533}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b95d3a6-7970-587e-a6e2-ec8585c4f125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363529} :answer-field/id #uuid "90beb5cf-42f0-5e02-8e07-82f4c8c55f21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "193b3ef5-bb74-515e-aa2f-4427c5bb5f15" :question/math-academy-id "q-165044" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+({t}^{2}-3)\\,j,$ $t\\in (0,\\infty ),$ find all points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-3)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\,i+({t}^{2}-3)\\,j & =c(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}i+2t\\,j) \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\,i+({t}^{2}-3)\\,j & =-\\frac{c}{{t}^{2}}i+2ct\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $i$ and $j$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{t} &=-\\frac{c}{{t}^{2}} \\\\ {t}^{2}-3 &=2ct\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation we obtain $-t=c.$ Substituting $c=-t$ into the second equality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-3 & =-2{t}^{2} \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-3 & =0 \\\\ 3(t+1)(t-1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\in (0,\\infty )$, the only solution is $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =\\frac{1}{1}\\,i+({1}^{2}-3)\\,j \\\\  & =\\,i-2\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(1,-2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363534 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1489818f-5ce9-545c-881f-dfa313c88d0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363536} {:answer/id #uuid "a863d5d6-4817-5d38-861e-294ff12c8daf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363537} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ac8a95-910b-5dff-900f-302d27cc3e92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363538} {:answer/id #uuid "41dd2e89-20f2-5f82-af5e-e4877efb589e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363539} {:answer/id #uuid "b75d956c-9807-5aca-a281-f664d5d8c25f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1489818f-5ce9-545c-881f-dfa313c88d0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363536} :answer-field/id #uuid "674aef77-64bb-5be9-9abf-cff3f0fb378e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "683e3791-c874-5158-8d10-18c20b047e9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-165417" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x{y}^{3}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot ({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{3} \\\\  & ={sin}^{7}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{7}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{7}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{7}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{8}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot {u}^{8}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363595 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78d66bb7-295d-5426-85d5-ecc60dac6869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78d66bb7-295d-5426-85d5-ecc60dac6869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363597} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a9dd6a1-550f-5ff1-ac0d-0d56b1c23d4f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363596} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a6b076b-e766-5c8f-a0cd-03e5ed12203c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a6b076b-e766-5c8f-a0cd-03e5ed12203c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363599} :answer-field/id #uuid "c21edd57-5874-58ec-99eb-20eab351adac" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363598} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "969f3547-f1ea-5d49-aedd-8aea07982cdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "969f3547-f1ea-5d49-aedd-8aea07982cdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363601} :answer-field/id #uuid "c69bc05e-3061-5ad4-a12b-c0cee73a6a62" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363600} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54767c70-f1e8-5c2a-9645-45bac744baa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54767c70-f1e8-5c2a-9645-45bac744baa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363603} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2abb45a-5a37-5e8a-8dec-08ca46eb293c" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363602} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59a6f51e-666c-56d3-ba03-4f7283a04750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59a6f51e-666c-56d3-ba03-4f7283a04750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363605} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b6eb381-0284-5836-85ce-d79a42d2b9b7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363604} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "482359e7-082a-504d-bc57-f6163c9f176a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "482359e7-082a-504d-bc57-f6163c9f176a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363607} :answer-field/id #uuid "61590ccc-fca1-55ef-a95d-40c03c2fae1e" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "290de3f9-01bf-5fef-a22f-da08c098a0ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-253323" :question/problem "Let ${J}_{n}(\\lambda )$ denote an $n\\times n$ Jordan block with $\\lambda $ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{3}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-6}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ $n=3,$ and $\\lambda =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nSince $n=3,$ we need to compute the first $3-1=2$ derivative of $f:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{3}(\\pi )) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & \\frac{1}{2!}{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ 0 & f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & {sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & {sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364061 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2502a117-f0a2-5d25-8266-26db174cee95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364063} {:answer/id #uuid "051966b0-a387-52dc-996f-11db19163995" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364064} {:answer/id #uuid "63109a39-0802-5e0b-9374-aef3582ed692" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2502a117-f0a2-5d25-8266-26db174cee95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364063} :answer-field/id #uuid "812da627-3b48-5d0c-9fa5-90a28cca8db4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364062} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6df08ed8-7546-5403-b5f9-bb8370a55738" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364067} {:answer/id #uuid "448bba3a-a057-5a71-8f39-3a480eeb7e43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364068} {:answer/id #uuid "07e6a9d8-e0ac-5246-8ad0-bdf8d6396476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "448bba3a-a057-5a71-8f39-3a480eeb7e43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364068} :answer-field/id #uuid "f938eca9-fd86-5840-a013-9fd0d292ce05" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2ec8b5c-545d-5b86-9913-1096df572a5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-339803" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(-2)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,-2\\le x\\le 0\n$$\n\nThe general solution can be written as\n\n$$\ny=f(x)+B\\cdot g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $B\\in R$ is arbitrary. \n\nSelect functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ that make this statement true.\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} \n$\\,g(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the second-order inhomogeneous boundary value problem (BVP)\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $P,Q,$ and $f$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and ${\\alpha }_{i}$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real constants such that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nIf the associated homogeneous BVP has nontrivial solutions, then the inhomogeneous BVP has either no solution or infinitely many solutions. \n\nTo find the solution of an inhomogeneous boundary value problem (BVP):\n\n- First, we find the general solution ${y}_{h}(x)$ of the corresponding homogeneous equation.\n\n- Then, we find a particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ of the inhomogeneous equation.\n\n- Finally, we apply the boundary conditions to determine the arbitrary constants.\n\nHowever, in this particular case, we can integrate the equation directly. By doing so, we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+Ax+B.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that the derivative ${y}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-x+A.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition ${y}^{′}(-2)=2$ gives\n\n$$\n2+A=2\\,⇒\\,A=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+B.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-0+A & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $B.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the boundary value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+B \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\right)+B(1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nf(x)=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2},\\,g(x)=1.\n$$\n\n \n\n**Note**: The associated homogeneous BVP\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0,\\,{y}^{′}(-2)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,-2\\le x\\le 0\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions. Therefore, the inhomogeneous BVP has either infinitely many solutions or no solution. Since we have found a solution that contains an arbitrary constant and satisfies both boundary conditions, the BVP has infinitely many solutions."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364330 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2548572d-1828-5c15-9c24-9f69c26499fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364332} {:answer/id #uuid "cdf16357-ff08-50aa-8d48-287a20aa338c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364333} {:answer/id #uuid "fa312d6c-985e-5953-8527-699e0ddc3fc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364334} {:answer/id #uuid "ccf0fdd7-7278-5aa7-85cf-08123b8757dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364335} {:answer/id #uuid "33458f8c-ced5-59e6-a323-8288679d085f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33458f8c-ced5-59e6-a323-8288679d085f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364336} :answer-field/id #uuid "d18620e4-b83a-5036-9cc0-164c3885d9ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "55c20ddf-7f41-5979-9443-af4fb8f03ef0" :question/math-academy-id "q-73342" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{-2}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2$ then what is ${\\int }_{-2}^{1}(5+4f(x))\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information into the above gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}+2={\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}={\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we compute the desired integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{1}(5+4f(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =5{\\int }_{-2}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x+4{\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\cdot x\\,{|}_{-2}^{1}+4\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =5(1-(-2))+10 \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364365 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e2d5a83-8718-5bdf-8eb3-db892e2ab5e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438532} {:answer/id #uuid "8eaaea36-3739-5e8a-a1ed-efc15f9bc00b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438533}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e2d5a83-8718-5bdf-8eb3-db892e2ab5e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438532} :answer-field/id #uuid "8efd7542-c77b-5d4d-8f60-5468e0925423" :answer-field/key "The system has no solution" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439295} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "763f0363-fe65-5380-acc8-6965de771643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438534} {:answer/id #uuid "be014b12-0b58-51d7-a7a3-d343e4554293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be014b12-0b58-51d7-a7a3-d343e4554293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438535} :answer-field/id #uuid "1338d41d-dfae-54bb-ada6-b58b1d8cc303" :answer-field/key "The system is consistent independent" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439296} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f48fb77e-36ef-5915-aaab-7df2660d984a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438536} {:answer/id #uuid "84b40478-f69c-539e-aac5-dd183a05b951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f48fb77e-36ef-5915-aaab-7df2660d984a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438536} :answer-field/id #uuid "7051a40c-c2de-5375-95a6-8801b39f67c5" :answer-field/key "The system is consistent dependent" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "35b0295d-1e16-59cc-a201-696e791e0b7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-74643" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+2y &=8 \\\\ x+y &=-2 \\\\ x+\\frac{1}{2}y &=5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first try to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 14 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 14 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 14 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ are solutions to the third equation $0x+0y=0$, we can remove this equation from the system. So, the corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y &=-2 \\\\ -y &=14.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=-14.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+(-14)=-2\\,⟹\\,x=12.\n$$\n\n \nSo,  we conclude that the system is consistent independent.   \n\nAs a result, only statement “The system is consistent independent” is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364399 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e48e2f8-a998-591f-85a7-74216c330826" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364401} {:answer/id #uuid "86c854f3-9b37-56bf-8ed0-d656aca9cb87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364402} {:answer/id #uuid "b16fdea2-eae9-5267-a3ec-033d8b940279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364403} {:answer/id #uuid "828d078c-1cec-5c53-b243-49495f238d47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364404} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a1bb85-3d86-5ec5-aae3-1f0c60397c3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86c854f3-9b37-56bf-8ed0-d656aca9cb87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364402} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e304ce4-5b90-51ac-8564-dc2bcdec4f03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3efc6eb6-1183-58cd-bd1a-3a29f39410eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-76614" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ along the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\theta ) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi -0}{4}-\\frac{0-0}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364406 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc4fe3ab-834d-54e6-8aa2-1f24a32b9e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi +1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364408} {:answer/id #uuid "11e0dcd6-b288-598a-ba1c-229ef1490ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi -1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364409} {:answer/id #uuid "beebdd75-a401-5283-81ff-5aa849295dce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi -2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364410} {:answer/id #uuid "377ccde4-480f-5a52-99b4-878b8324b114" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +2}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364411} {:answer/id #uuid "ef48135e-5181-5bd2-954a-39061d970853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi +2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "377ccde4-480f-5a52-99b4-878b8324b114" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +2}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364411} :answer-field/id #uuid "49a9a65b-096a-5622-a126-8ea061e266fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "088ac295-32da-54ad-8397-ade18f5cdf50" :question/math-academy-id "q-76683" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, where $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le 0.$ \n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & ={cos}^{4}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{0}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{0}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{0}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{0}\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{4}{|}_{-\\pi /4}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(0-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi +2}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364413 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49adc8e9-1dc5-5cb5-9175-f91e269efe35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "192" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364415} {:answer/id #uuid "e94aff50-1cf2-58b3-85e2-e76471a133a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364416} {:answer/id #uuid "7fdf46cb-8dea-538a-974a-83bf0db567f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "256" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364417} {:answer/id #uuid "119dacc9-0e3b-5285-87d0-21d3cc26e860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "512" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364418} {:answer/id #uuid "bddd3fe2-39af-5dc8-a534-e22068d4a73c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "384" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49adc8e9-1dc5-5cb5-9175-f91e269efe35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "192" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364415} :answer-field/id #uuid "7944764b-8f2e-517b-9311-deccaafc6156" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13c81ebd-eba4-55aa-a40f-bfa1b49cc9df" :question/math-academy-id "q-76763" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}y$ along the polar curve $r=4$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{3}y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{3}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =256{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(4)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}256{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot 4\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\,024{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\,024{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =-1\\,024{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-1\\,024\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{u}^{4}\\,{|}_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}/2} \\\\  & =-256(\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)^{4}-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =-256\\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =192\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364620 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87e36d9c-e9a2-5446-ad66-2c509425fb2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364622} {:answer/id #uuid "6838046c-6bb6-5382-aff4-79052bb83389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364623} {:answer/id #uuid "66729efb-cb03-5e0d-975f-24a9597e8440" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364624} {:answer/id #uuid "0e78a432-6e13-5220-8bcd-195f0ec965d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364625} {:answer/id #uuid "eac12345-022f-5cbe-aa4a-767d1ed83136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66729efb-cb03-5e0d-975f-24a9597e8440" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364624} :answer-field/id #uuid "736f5b90-2f10-5a15-8216-cf8f1586fc1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5c7952d1-1f1c-50a2-a342-5271912d43de" :question/math-academy-id "q-96033" :question/problem "$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{22}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\left(-\\frac{6}{18}\\right)\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{22}=\\left(\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\right)\\cdot (3\\sqrt{2})=4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364627 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7983aa7b-5216-54f6-b050-58815a2ab40f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364629} {:answer/id #uuid "d666a00e-b87c-596f-ab51-91aea6a4472b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364630} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e175c7-d060-594c-a3a4-0d00100f671b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364631} {:answer/id #uuid "cfba2c96-3a0e-57ea-9a97-ef324f21b60e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364632} {:answer/id #uuid "751bc3a2-968b-51b3-9ba2-0fdee249461f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "751bc3a2-968b-51b3-9ba2-0fdee249461f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364633} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f87d5ad-b6ae-5eab-a689-b6a81915da03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "36d5df8c-7b50-5acf-8853-a8f4a4bedaea" :question/math-academy-id "q-96034" :question/problem "$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{22}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{6}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{22}=\\left(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\right)\\cdot (\\sqrt{6})=-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364749 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "544ecd2d-bde7-5531-b6ed-1f7d10a3f9e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364751} {:answer/id #uuid "cab4f01d-178a-546b-bd23-3fe90d910fbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364752} {:answer/id #uuid "71a1d39e-5367-5159-b5e6-12901992c3c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{3}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364753} {:answer/id #uuid "dc508202-a859-5fdd-a4a8-38f77d73654b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364754} {:answer/id #uuid "b7d064ef-4a9d-55d2-9e65-78879a8a071e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364755}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc508202-a859-5fdd-a4a8-38f77d73654b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364754} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf4dec28-6ad5-5a64-90ef-6978e1019e65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b0f3d46-2a1f-5dae-8237-79287f94cce2" :question/math-academy-id "q-109331" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\sqrt{y-3}$ that lies between the lines $y=3$ and $y=\\frac{15}{4}.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt{y-3})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y-3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4(y-3)}.\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{3}^{15/4}\\sqrt{y-3}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4(y-3)}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{3}^{15/4}\\sqrt{y-3}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{4y-11}{4(y-3)}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{3}^{15/4}\\sqrt{4y-11}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=4y-11.$ Then, $\\text{d}y=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits:\n\n| **$y$** | $3$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\\pi {\\int }_{3}^{15/4}\\sqrt{4y-11}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {[\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(\\sqrt{{4}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{1}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(8-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364756 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "985e3977-3e44-511f-9f39-9e2d482ed5ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{1+{(-3{x}^{2}+8x-4)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364758} {:answer/id #uuid "fabdc79a-55e7-5166-93ca-43d3d190fd99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{(-{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-4x)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364759} {:answer/id #uuid "04ca053c-9deb-5648-a23c-c40e6457e67d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\sqrt{1+{(-{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-4x)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364760} {:answer/id #uuid "4bec0b3c-eecc-5d40-85bf-665176173794" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{(-3{x}^{2}+8x-4)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364761} {:answer/id #uuid "7170c244-4e73-5215-bdf5-a6177e90d079" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x(-3{x}^{2}+8x-4)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "985e3977-3e44-511f-9f39-9e2d482ed5ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{1+{(-3{x}^{2}+8x-4)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364758} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cf3c163-585a-58e8-bdd3-011ef184838b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9f152892-0c57-54cf-824f-ee3392d9b983" :question/math-academy-id "q-109425" :question/problem "![](images/21/21b55135fd1fbd0c3120b19ee6c4688dea2d8b13ccfe842e95262c87fb0bf6e0.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $y=-{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-4x$ that lies in the fourth quadrant between its $x$-intercepts. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-4x & =0 \\\\ -x({x}^{2}-4x+4) & =0 \\\\ -x(x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-4x)=-3{x}^{2}+8x-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{1+{(-3{x}^{2}+8x-4)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364887 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9aeba58e-2480-5903-9203-c20e42f54324" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=37.5\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=5\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364889} {:answer/id #uuid "8a0694e3-2d64-5b6e-8fdf-99e203f46da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=38\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=5\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364890} {:answer/id #uuid "145e16e0-16a5-5047-a144-2563881addf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=14\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=1\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364891} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd66380-ff8b-5bf8-a8b5-4ec3bda201de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=24.5\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=2\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364892} {:answer/id #uuid "8d9f848b-1702-55cc-8d51-da43e9b161cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=75\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=5\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a0694e3-2d64-5b6e-8fdf-99e203f46da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=38\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=5\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364890} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d2fe126-317a-5c9d-9ee4-ef8f3e825381" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b21641d-ae30-55b8-ba2d-d86f82ac4d9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-120784" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=15-3t,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=14\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity." :question/worked-solution "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 15-3t & =0 \\\\ t & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $t=5\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(15-3t) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(5)<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=5\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (15-3t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =15t-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1)=14.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15(1)-\\frac{3}{2}(1)^{2}+C & =14 \\\\ C & =\\frac{3}{2}-1\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{1}{2}+15t-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}.$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{max}}=v(5)=\\frac{1}{2}+15(5)-\\frac{3}{2}(5)^{2}=38\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364920 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2a94f5e-8e68-549e-a8ed-83dc42cea847" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x-{x}^{2})\\sqrt{5-4x+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364922} {:answer/id #uuid "ac0fd73d-dd4f-5970-ad42-69fec767d6be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\sqrt{5-4x+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364923} {:answer/id #uuid "ead93f82-75e9-5fdd-b570-be82cb5d48e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{5-8x+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364924} {:answer/id #uuid "b85abca9-7ac1-5deb-9121-e5e5d0b99371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{5-8x+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364925} {:answer/id #uuid "97dabfcf-1d3b-5c66-893e-01e7a3df3a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x-{x}^{2})\\sqrt{5-8x+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b85abca9-7ac1-5deb-9121-e5e5d0b99371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{5-8x+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364925} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee10a059-aa95-593d-a0f7-1d56ebfcbda6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3bc1fc15-2cf1-50bc-bcb4-3171e2123e4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-126714" :question/problem "![](images/0b/0b69c23833e64df8fa1c938bb87b6cfcf8a67018fb1af73424d26e64e49ee4f5.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $y=2x-{x}^{2}$ that lies in the first quadrant between its $x$-intercepts. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x(2-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x-{x}^{2})=2-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{1+{(2-2x)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{1+4-8x+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{5-8x+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364927 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8109b0a-084e-505e-9677-6cf31816c39a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}\\,\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364929} {:answer/id #uuid "dc3b6592-41e1-5b53-94ef-adc717ecca52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364930} {:answer/id #uuid "867fe616-bbda-594a-bfee-71689e98ac0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364931} {:answer/id #uuid "b7cb1890-ceb3-525c-9a55-9c6ad9e148bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{28\\sqrt{2}\\,\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364932} {:answer/id #uuid "0c7ca7ae-4d0c-52bf-ab6d-6e2d8df75e36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7cb1890-ceb3-525c-9a55-9c6ad9e148bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{28\\sqrt{2}\\,\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364932} :answer-field/id #uuid "14229d88-ad4c-5463-b8a3-92a65fb233c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c6bfdd4b-1abf-5cab-8f44-31d764fee083" :question/math-academy-id "q-126730" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\sqrt{1-2y}$ that lies between the lines $y=-3$ and $y=0.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt{1-2y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{2\\sqrt{1-2y}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-2y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{1}{1-2y}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{0}\\sqrt{1-2y}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{1-2y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{0}\\sqrt{1-2y}\\sqrt{\\frac{2-2y}{1-2y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{0}\\sqrt{1-y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\pi {[-\\frac{2}{3}(1-y)^{3/2}]}_{-3}^{0} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\pi (-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{16}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{28\\sqrt{2}\\,\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364941 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22b110c6-9fd5-576d-9f5b-275ce0d70db3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\,i-\\frac{2}{3}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364943} {:answer/id #uuid "6ed75cab-f2d8-58b4-8474-83308e971b32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\,i-\\frac{2}{3}\\,j-\\frac{2}{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364944} {:answer/id #uuid "1126d603-8ddc-50e0-b1c7-0654ca022b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\,j-\\frac{1}{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364945} {:answer/id #uuid "8ad74be8-da86-55d8-b8a1-77a8d96f3d02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}\\,j-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364946} {:answer/id #uuid "5aaacd48-c1e7-5856-9575-7beafa4902dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,i-\\frac{2}{3}\\,j-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ed75cab-f2d8-58b4-8474-83308e971b32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\,i-\\frac{2}{3}\\,j-\\frac{2}{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364944} :answer-field/id #uuid "fea2b633-2caf-5b5f-8fa5-c0931244ee30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b5b234d3-95b5-5e8d-a6f9-df8675102205" :question/math-academy-id "q-127298" :question/problem "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\,p(t)$ at the point corresponding to $t=0$, given that  $p(0)=2\\,i+j-k\\,$ and $\\,{p}^{′}(0)=i-2\\,j-2\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)i+y(t)j+z(t)k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\,p(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\right)\\cdot p(t)+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\,p(t)+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\,{p}^{′}(t) \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{2(0)}{{({0}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\,p(0)+\\frac{1}{{0}^{2}+1}\\,{p}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =0(2\\,i+j-k)+(\\,i-2\\,j-2k) \\\\  & =\\,i-2\\,j-2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(0):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖r(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(0):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(0)}{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (i-2\\,j-2\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,i-\\frac{2}{3}\\,j-\\frac{2}{3}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=0$ is  $T(0)=\\frac{1}{3}\\,i-\\frac{2}{3}\\,j-\\frac{2}{3}\\,k.$"}]
